      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Indio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1907 Indio opened the USDA’s Date Station, sparking a boom that now supplies 41.4 million pounds of dates annually.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.indio.org/our-city",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Farmers irrigated the desert with wells and later the All‑American Canal, allowing onions, cotton, grapes, citrus, and dates to flourish.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.indio.org/our-city",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every year Indio hosts the National Date Festival, a Middle Eastern‑themed celebration that nods to the crop’s origins.",
